Grace

Thanks for sharing figures of your data.  I'm afraid I would be reluctant to make an inference about the density of animals in the bushland having seen the histogram.  I wonder if your animals are detecting you before you detect them in the more open (?) bushland.  Clearly a decreasing detection function will not fit the bushland (or non-type).  Note the open circles marching across the top of your bottom left figure.  I suspect that the "detection function" fitted to your non-type detections; implying that detectability in that non-type habitat is perfect.  Certainly that statement lacks credibility.

If you believe detectability on the line is perfect (g(0)=1), then you could rely upon pooling robustness to say you are getting a good estimate for the entire study area, even if there are differences in detectability between habitats.  But perhaps a post-stratification covariate analysis might not be warranted because of the awkwardness of the bushland detections.

