Subject: underestimation of abundance in a dsm
Message-ID: <__OCI_Q9New.tdRFMHNNFgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/__OCI_Q9New/m/tdRFMHNNFgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all
We are working to estimate the spatial abundance of sheep in a ranch using DSM approach. We have a scale of segment and grid of 500m side, and a total of 74 segments. We achieve models with good residuals that explain up to 60% of the deviance, using variables such as EVI (vegetation index), distance to the watering hole, using the paddock (= ranch divisions) as a factor. Our problem arises when we make predictions, which result in very low values (about 500 animals), compared with the sheep stock that we know the ranch has (about 2500). The total CVs (df +GAM) are high (between 0.30 and 0.50) but even so the value of the upper limit of the confidence interval of the estimate does not include nor is it close to 2500 animals. The spatial distribution of the abundances predicted with these models represents very well the expected distribution of the sheep in the field, i.e. the relative abundance seems to be fine.
We believe that we do not have overfitting in the variables since the edf are low.  We have a good range of representativeness of the predictor variables between segdata and our prediction grid. We calculate abundance with the classic distance sampling approach (based on design) and that gives us about 3000 animals, quite close to the real value, which we believe suggests that the problem is not our sampling design.

What do you think is happening?
Any advice will be very helpfull!

Thanks in advance
Natalia

From ub4370a397bda3bc4 at groups.google.com  Sat Sep  7 13:01:09 2019
From: ub4370a397bda3bc4 at groups.google.com (Ryan Welsh)
Date: Sat, 07 Sep 2019 13:01:09 +0000
Subject: Introducing uncertainty into animal indentification during transects
Message-ID: <_i4jZcLpcj0.Vq-1oyKUFgAJ@distance-sampling.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/_i4jZcLpcj0/m/Vq-1oyKUFgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I have been working on putting together some Distance sampling and DSMs on a foraging ground used by Green Turtles in the Florida Keys, and I have come across a possibly unique problem when looking at the data.  These grounds have two size classes that use the foraging grounds, these size classes are usually delineated by the length of the shell, (< 85 cm is a sub-adult, > 85cm is a adult) when the animals is captured.  Something we do not do during our transect surveys, so there exists some uncertainty when we are identifying green turtles by size class during our transect surveys.

However, once transect surveys are done we do capture animals on these foraging grounds, and when we do we can look at what our initial call of size class when the animal was sighted before capture, and then what the subsequent shell measurement tells us the size class actually was.  Through this, we know we are correct about 80% of the time.  Is there a way to incorporate this uncertainty into the modelling?

TL/DR: When identifying animals to size class during surveys we know there is about a 20% chance there is mis-identification happening, is it possible to incorporate this into Distance?

thanks,
Ryan

